AB  - Climate change has intensified the global demand for cooling solutions, particularly in regions experiencing extreme heatwaves. Kinetic building façades act as a shading structure, dynamically adjusting their shape according to environmental conditions to improve energy efficiency and occupant comfort. However, traditional application has largely been limited to commercial and office buildings due to high upfront costs and complex maintenance. This paper presents a lower cost, prefabricated, data-driven alternative designed for home units. The structure integrates real-time environmental sensing with predictive controls to adjust shading components in response to changing environments. Simulation results show that the proposed approach outperforms static and rule-based scenarios, achieving a 12% reduction in cooling demand during summer months compared to the static baseline. This finding demonstrates the potential of accessible adaptive shading systems to mitigate heat stress while decrease cooling energy use in residential contexts. © 2026 ASHRAE.
