AD  - Department of Arch. Technology, College of Fine Art, University of Tehran, Iran
AB  - This study presents the Algae Autobioreactor (AAB), an advanced photo-bioreactor system designed for architectural integration as windows, building facades, and shading elements. We developed the Real-time Image Processing Automation of the Algae Panel (RIPAAP) system to enable continuous monitoring of critical parameters including algal concentration, density, Visible Transmittance (VT), Solar Heat Gain Coefficient (SHGC), and U-value. Unlike conventional approaches limited to static algal concentrations, our method captures dynamic growth patterns over extended periods. The dynamic growth patterns were analyzed in two distinct configurations: Case 1 (vertical slats) and Case 2 (horizontal slats), representing fundamentally different shading approaches. Experimental results demonstrate distinct behavioral patterns: in Case 1, initial concentrations of 20–30 % exhibited rapid fluctuations between 40–90 % during subsequent days before stabilizing, accompanied by reduced VT and SHGC variability. Case 2 showed similar initial behavior but maintained higher concentration volatility through day 4. Quantitative analysis revealed VT variations of 0.05–0.18 (40–80 % concentration) and SHGC ranges of 0.09–0.20 for Case 1, while Case 2 displayed VT changes of 0.02–0.17 (40–90 % concentration) with SHGC values of 0.02–0.21. The RIPAAP system's ability to track these transient phenomena in real-time represents a significant advancement over traditional measurement techniques, offering new capabilities for optimizing both Energy Efficiency (EE) and Human Health (HH) parameters in building-integrated bioreactor systems. These findings establish a foundation for dynamic control of algal-based architectural elements and demonstrate the critical relationship between concentration changes and optical/thermal performance metrics. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
