AD  - Architectural Intelligence Group, Department of Architecture and Civil Engineering, City University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of China, China
AB  - As existing buildings are increasingly retrofitted in response to frequently changing demands, the configuration of façade systems becomes critical for ensuring adaptability to evolving functional use and climate conditions. While modular façades offer this potential, current approaches remain case-specific and simulation-intensive, constraining early-stage exploration of scenario-based design and performance-driven optimization. This study proposes an image-to-image surrogate framework that couples a generative adversarial network (GAN) with multi-objective optimization to support design optimization of modular façades in open-plan offices. Using a university educational building in Singapore as a case study, each façade opening-ratio maps (12 × 18 panels) and corresponding annual fields of daylight autonomy (DA), glare autonomy (GA), and thermal comfort percentage (TCP) are generated and encoded as 256 × 256 grayscale images for model training. The calibrated surrogate model accurately reproduces spatial performance patterns and reduces per-evaluation runtime by nearly 90% compared to simulations. Scenario experiments across three representative layouts demonstrate that surrogate-based single and multi-objective optimization decreases combined the root mean square error (RMSE) by 13.7–21.7%, while designer-specified inputs increase errors by up to 57.1% in complex layouts. The results highlight that data-driven façade configurations better capture thermo-optical trade-offs than experience-based schemes. The proposed framework offers a scalable, visually spatial decision-support tool for adaptive modular façade retrofits and can be extended to additional climates, geometries, and performance objectives. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
