AD  - Department of Civil, Construction and Environmental Engineering, Iowa State University, United States
AD  - Department of Mechanical and Nuclear Engineering, Kansas State University, United States
AD  - Department of Aerospace Engineering, Iowa State University, United States
AB  - Modern tall and slender structures often experience significant wind-induced vibrations (WIV). This study explores the effectiveness of a smart morphing façade system featuring motorized vertical corner plates to control WIV in high-rise buildings. A novel cyber-physical framework is proposed and validated through wind tunnel experiments using a 1:76 scaled aeroelastic building model. The influence of surrounding buildings on wind turbulence is also considered, ensuring adaptability to dynamic urban environments. To enable real-time responsiveness and optimize plate orientations, the system integrates an encoder-decoder Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) model and a Genetic Reinforcement Learning (GRL) algorithm. The GRU model defines the system state using data from pressure probes, accelerometers, and atmospheric sensors. At each control interval, the GRL algorithm leverages past plate angles and GRU states to train multi-layer perceptron (MLP) policies. It combines genetic algorithms (GAs) with random search to balance exploration and exploitation while optimizing future plate angles. The results show significant reductions in root mean square (RMS) accelerations, up to 50% compared to the uncontrolled model. Frequency domain analysis also reveals a decrease in energy around dominant frequencies, confirming the system's effectiveness. This represents a pioneering advancement in the development of smart morphing facade systems that adapt to wind conditions and mitigate structural vibrations efficiently. © 2026
