TI  - Predicting emotional impact of adaptive façades through Machine Learning, A VR-based study with PAD assessment
PY  - 2026
T2  - Building and Environment
VL  - 297
C7  - 114541
DO  - 10.1016/j.buildenv.2026.114541
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034360495?origin=resultslist
AD  - Ion Mincu University of Architecture and Urbanism, Bucharest, 010014, Romania
AD  - Umberto Alibrandi, National University of Singapore, Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering
AD  - Aarhus University, Dept Civil and Architectural Engineering, Aarhus, 8000, Denmark
AB  - As global mental health concerns rise and indoor environments dominate daily life, understanding how architecture shapes emotional well-being has become urgent. Adaptive façades hold promise for creating responsive environments, yet their emotional impact remains poorly understood due to complex relationships between façade parameters and subjective responses. This study establishes a Machine Learning framework to assess architecture's emotional impact through adaptive shading effects. Using a Virtual Reality experiment, the Pleasure, Arousal, and Dominance (PAD) emotional responses were collected from 111 participants exposed to dynamic façade configurations, varying parameters such as speed, spatial spread, and coverage ratio. Challenges of subjective data were addressed through consensus-based classification and probabilistic modelling, evaluating multiple algorithms based on KL Divergence and Ranked Probability Score. Random Forest emerged optimal, achieving 85% accuracy for pleasure prediction in consensus analysis and 75% top-k accuracy in probabilistic modelling. Participant clustering improved performance to 87% for pleasure, while arousal proved most challenging to predict. Shapley Additive Explanations revealed spread, speed, and coverage ratio as most influential parameters. Optimal design zones occur at moderate speeds (<0.1 m/s) with medium spread ratios (0-10. Such configurations can describe low-distraction dynamic louvers or blinds, supporting calm atmosphere in common working, learning or relaxing spaces. The findings provide transferable, implementation-agnostic, perceptual design principles and parameter thresholds for dynamic shading in adaptive façades. By embracing uncertainty rather than averaging it out, the probabilistic framework offers adaptive systems that respect user diversity. Results advance integration of emotional considerations into evidence-based architectural practice. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
