AD  - STEM College, School of Engineering, RMIT University, VIC, Melbourne, 3001, Australia
AB  - Contemporary building design increasingly incorporates adaptive envelope systems, such as external louvered blinds, to respond to environmental stimuli and enhance thermal comfort while managing indoor airflow. This study investigates the use of bimetallic materials as passive actuators, integrated with aluminium substrates, to drive thermally responsive louver movement. Three design parameters are examined: bimetal coating percentages, bimetal layers, and aluminium base thickness. Twelve material configurations were evaluated using nonlinear thermo-mechanical finite element analysis, with results validated by laboratory experiments. It demonstrated that solar-induced heating raised the bimetal-integrated aluminium surface temperature by approximately 1–15 °C above ambient, producing free-end flap displacements consistent with Finite Element Method (FEM) predictions. The influence of louver movement on indoor air velocity was analyzed using coupled Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) simulations across five coastal Australian cities with diverse climatic conditions. The results demonstrated that reducing the aluminium plate thickness and applying at least 70 % bimetal coating increased flap displacement, exceeding 3 mm, reaching up to 5 mm. Elevated ambient temperatures led to larger deflections and enhanced indoor air velocities, improving passive ventilation performance in warmer climates. This research offers valuable design guidance for optimizing adaptive louver systems to improve natural ventilation and reduce energy consumption in residential buildings. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
