AD  - Department of Building Science, Tsinghua University, Beijing, 100084, China
AD  - Key Laboratory of Eco Planning & Green Building, Ministry of Education, Tsinghua University, Beijing, 100084, China
AD  - CSCEC JIUHE Development Group Co. Ltd., Beijing, 100070, China
AB  - Extreme climates heighten the need for climate adaptation in low-carbon buildings. Taking thermally variable solar collection walls with flat plate gravity heat pipes as an example, the study explores the effects of geometric parameters and the structure’s radiative characteristics on building energy efficiency. Results showed that increasing the thickness can increase the wall’s heat transfer capacity by 45.0 W/m2 (corresponding to a 36.0%). Still, the increase in the effective heat transfer length shows a negative correlation, and the evaporation section may even decrease by 52.4 W (15.8% of the total). Heat collection performance varied by orientation and season, with the west façade accumulating the most heat in summer and the south façade in winter. Optimizing the evaporation section’s emissivity can increase annual heat collection by 132.57 kW·h (19.49% increase). Electrochromic cover glazing can decrease the annual heat collection by 147.29–994.12 kW·h. Based on the cross-region comparison, the winter heating energy-saving rates in Lhasa, Beijing, and Harbin are 73.08%, 6.42%, and 1.23%, and increases in summer cooling energy consumption are 8.23%, 1.01%, and 2.20%, respectively. Findings can support the development of climate-adaptive building envelopes. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier B.V.
