TI  - Wind-driven rain mitigation effects of building facade shading systems: insights from comparative field measurements
PY  - 2026
T2  - Building and Environment
VL  - 301
C7  - 114752
DO  - 10.1016/j.buildenv.2026.114752
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105039786699?origin=resultslist
AD  - South China University of Technology School of Architecture, China
AB  - Wind-driven rain (WDR) represents a notable concern for building envelopes and indoor environmental quality in hot and humid regions. Although field measurements have characterized WDR distribution patterns on facades, the role of building facade shading systems in WDR mitigation remains underexplored. Building on our previous finding that the buffer zone formed by overhangs can effectively mitigate WDR, this study investigated the modulation mechanisms and mitigation effects of a shading system comprising overhangs and vertical louvers. Synchronized high-resolution wind and rain data were collected via field measurements to compare the shading system with both the directly exposed zone and the buffer zone. Results show that the shading system substantially altered the near-wall flow field, reduced the wind speed inside the shading system by 74.4%–83.9% and shifted the three-dimensional turbulence intensity ratio from 1:0.7:0.4 to 1:0.8:0.6. Raindrop behavior suggests size sorting and breakup: medium raindrops (0.937–2.215 mm) exhibited the highest interception efficiency; small raindrops (<0.562 mm) were deflected by the airflow; large raindrops (>3.75 mm) penetrated due to inertial momentum. Wind speed and rainfall intensity exhibited a significant negative synergy on the WDR mitigation rate. Quantitative analysis shows that the shading system achieves overall WDR mitigation rates of 80% relative to the directly exposed zone and 40% relative to the buffer zone, with the effect strongly dependent on raindrop diameter. This study enhances both empirical and theoretical understanding of WDR mitigation by shading systems, providing a scientific foundation for climate-adaptive facade design. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
