AB  - Climate change has recently intensified extreme wind-rain events in China's hot-humid regions, where wind-driven rain (WDR) critically impacts building envelope durability. While research investigating the effects of WDR on building surfaces is extensive, the influence of facade design morphology remains underexplored. These regions widely employ climate-adaptive facade buffer zones exhibiting strong WDR mitigation potential. However, quantitative studies on wind-rain distribution and mitigation effects within such zones remain limited, thus further investigation is required to clarify WDR mitigation mechanisms. In this study, a sixth-floor buffer zone unit within a multistory building in Guangzhou was examined. Wind-rain parameters were measured in exterior and buffer zones, yielding high-resolution datasets during three short-duration rainfall events in 2025. The results indicated that the buffer zone reduced the mean wind speed (WS) by 65%–81% and altered the turbulence intensity ratio from 1.0:0.9:0.4 (Iu: Iv: Iw) to 1.0:0.7:0.6. Rain intensity (RI) mitigation rate decreased from 83.7% to 71.0% as RI increased. Small raindrops (<0.562 mm) accumulated because of airflow disturbance, whereas larger raindrops exhibited unimodal retention. The gamma distribution fit midrange raindrops (0.562–2.375 mm) well but deviated at the extremes. Critical thresholds for WDR mitigation efficiency (η) were identified at WS <7.05 m/s or RI <7.04 mm/h, with a synergistic suppression effect observed between WS and RI (R² = 0.806; RMSE = 0.11). The findings demonstrate that η depends on WS, RI, and raindrop size distribution (RSD), offering essential theoretical and practical support for designing WDR-resistant and climate-adaptive buildings under extreme weathers. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
