AB  - Daily heating and cooling requirements generate approximately 50% of the global total energy demand for buildings. The exploration of active thermal management for buildings that dynamically responding to variable environments including seasonal changes, daily time variations, and solar irradiation with low energy consumption is of high importance. Inspired by the dynamic deformation responses of Mimosa pudica to light, we present kinetic reversible building facades with stacked anisotropic thermal conductivity and radiation-regulation layers that facilitate radiative heating and cooling. In response to ambient solar radiation, the deformation of the facades via a “cell-like-hinge” based on liquid–vapor phase changing achieves intelligent switching between solar heating and radiative cooling. Compared with present adaptive facades based on programing materials, the working temperature and response of our facades can be modulated simply in a wide range by grayscale of hinge with double self-weight load-bearing capacities to drive thermal regulation. The device achieves ∼40.80 W m−2 of average cooling power in hot and ∼531.4 W m−2 of average heating power in cold without any external energy consumption during the whole process. Coupled with heat-flow adjustment in accordance with the anisotropic thermal conductivity, a temperature reduction of 5 °C at an ambient temperature of 33 °C was achieved in experimental scale. Building energy simulation predicts quantitatively our prototype of dual-mode reversible building facades represents efficient radiative thermal-management strategy toward applying in a scalable and cost-efficient buildings with dynamic daily and seasonal variations. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
