AD  - İzmir Institute of Technology, Department of Architecture, Gülbahçe Campus, Urla, İzmir, 35430, Turkey
AB  - This study addresses the challenge of improving building envelope performance in Mediterranean climates, where dynamic seasonal changes require innovative solutions. The review examines the potential of high-performance building envelope applications, including building-integrated photovoltaics (BIPVs), shading devices, and vertical green walls, to enhance energy efficiency, comfort, and minimize environmental impact. While previous studies have predominantly focused on individual systems, this review considers a holistic approach by examining both separate and combined applications. The findings indicate that BIPV systems significantly enhance energy production, while shading devices mitigate overheating and enhance thermal and visual comfort. Additionally, vertical green walls enhance building performance by providing insulation and contributing to environmental benefits. The integration of these systems demonstrates synergistic potential to achieve multiple performance objectives, including energy savings, enhanced indoor comfort, and reduced emissions. However, the review identifies critical gaps in the literature, such as a lack of integration of holistic systems, limited consideration of future climate scenarios, and underutilization of optimization algorithms and machine learning in high-performance building envelope studies. Future research should lead to the development of adaptive design strategies, modular façade systems, holistic use of various applications, and incorporation of future climate predictions to ensure long-term sustainability. This study highlights the importance of developing integrated, high-performance building envelopes that adapt to changing environmental and climatic conditions. In conclusion, this review offers a comprehensive framework for advancing sustainable building practices in Mediterranean climates, providing valuable insights for architects and engineers seeking to create energy-efficient and resilient built environments. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
