AD  - International Research Center for Green Building and Low-Carbon City, Zhejiang University, International Campus, Haining, 314400, China
AB  - The building envelope has a significant effect on its overall energy consumption. Integrating advanced techniques, such as phase change material (PCM), dynamic insulation materials and systems (DIMS), and pipe-embedded technology, into envelope design can significantly reduce heating and cooling loads. However, the energy-saving performance of these technologies varies considerably across different climatic conditions. In this study, five wall types incorporating various combinations of PCM, DIMS, and embedded pipes were modeled in COMSOL to evaluate their thermal performance and climate adaptability across different regions. In Hangzhou, results showed that during peak summer, PCM alone had a limited effect. In contrast, dynamic insulation achieved much higher savings, and the combined system reduced cooling load by up to 84%, showing the most significant potential. Further analysis revealed that different wall types exhibit varying sensitivities to climatic conditions, with day temperature having the most critical impact, explaining over 69 % of the cooling load variance in non-pipe-embedded walls. Regression analysis quantified the sensitivity of each wall type to key climatic parameters, and clustering analysis identified the most applicable wall type for each climate zone. Across 30 cities, cooling load reductions varied greatly, ranging from below 5 % to nearly 100 %, across different climatic groups, highlighting the strong climate dependence of wall applicability. The study quantitatively links climatic parameters with wall performance, providing a rational basis for climate-adaptive envelope design. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd.
