AB  - Vertical Building-Integrated Photovoltaics (BIPV) offer a promising pathway for decarbonizing high-rise buildings, yet optimizing the trade-off between power generation and thermal load management remains challenging in Hot Summer and Cold Winter (HSCW) climates. This study proposes a vertical single-axis tracking PV louver system and establishes a hybrid simulation framework that couples Radiance for optical-electrical modeling with a high-fidelity XGBoost surrogate thermal model to predict annual building performance efficiently. Taking a representative office in Chengdu as a case study, the research systematically evaluates the energy impacts of array density, static tilt angles, and dynamic tracking strategies. Results demonstrate that maximizing louver density yields optimal net energy performance despite increased mutual shading. Compared to the optimal fixed-angle configuration (EUI = 56.1 kWh/m2), introducing solar tracking significantly reduces energy consumption. The Rule-Based Dynamic System achieved the theoretical minimum EUI of 44.1 kWh/m2, representing a 59.4% reduction relative to the baseline. The results show that, while solar tracking maximizes power generation by aligning panels perpendicular to the low-angle winter sun, it simultaneously acts as a shading barrier that obstructs beneficial passive heat gain, exacerbating heating loads. However, optimization analysis reveals that the Rule-Based Dynamic System defaults to the solar-tracking mode for the vast majority of the year, indicating that the value of electricity generation outweighs passive heat gain during most operational hours. Consequently, the performance advantage of the complex Dynamic System over the continuous Pure Solar-Tracking System (EUI = 44.3 kWh/m2) is marginal (0.2 kWh/m2). Considering the diminishing returns of acquiring passive heat versus high-grade electricity in cooling-dominated regions, the Pure Solar-Tracking System is recommended as the most robust and pragmatically optimal solution for vertical facades. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
