AB  - With rising demands for building energy conservation and indoor quality, photovoltaic shading facades (PVSFs) attract attention for radiation control and power generation. However, current designs struggle with synergistic optimization, photovoltaic (PV) self-shading, and high actuation energy. Existing analytical frameworks are constrained by neglecting view quality (VQ), decision redundancy, and insufficient mechanism analysis. Therefore, inspired by kiri-origami, this study proposes a novel segmented dynamic PVSF that utilizes single-sided stretching to achieve partitioned collaborative optimization (upper light guiding, lower PV shading) while mitigating PV self-shading. Concurrently, a framework integrating interpretable machine learning (IML) and criteria importance through intercriteria correlation (CRITIC) analyzes PVSF improvements in VQ, energy consumption, and daylighting. Results indicate the static PVSF selected via multi-objective decision making (MODM) maintains low PV self-shading (5.7%) and limited view obstruction, increasing useful daylight illuminance (UDI100–3000) by 7.4% and reducing net energy use intensity (net EUI) by 19.9% versus baseline. Furthermore, dynamic seasonal strategies (larger summer/smaller winter stretching angles) further optimize UDI100–3000, net EUI, VQ, and PV self-shading by 1.2%, 4.9%, 4.4%, and 7%, respectively. Subsequent climate adaptability analysis indicates that the system maintains low PV self-shading and optimizes daylighting and energy consumption in different climates, with dynamic strategies further enhancing performance. Finally, sensitivity analysis reveals that across climate zones (2A-6A), shading density gradually emerges as the dominant factor for energy efficiency, while component distribution replaces it as the primary factor for daylighting. Thus, this system offers a viable pathway for building-integrated photovoltaics (BIPV), while the framework enhances decision-making accuracy. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
