AB  - In the grinding process, measuring the profile of the grinding wheel is important for evaluating the accuracy of machining the workpiece, monitoring the wear of the grinding wheel and compensating for machining errors. Vision-based monitoring methods are more accurate and intuitive than traditional methods. Therefore, this paper proposes an automatic grinding wheel profile monitoring method based on the dynamic wheel envelope. A sub-pixel edge detection method based on omnidirectional gradient is proposed, which expands the gradient direction of the image to any direction by constructing a variable image window. The high-precision localization of sub-pixel edges is achieved by normalizing the window, bringing the edge detection accuracy to the sub-pixel level. The idea of dimensionality reduction and parameter space decomposition is introduced into the Hough circle transformation to obtain outer circle pixels for coarse edge localization, and then the Tukey weight function is used to iteratively fit the circle to locate the precise edge of the dynamic grinding wheel envelope. A dynamic real-time detection algorithm for grinding wheel wear was implemented through the design of an omnidirectional gradient sub-pixel edge detection algorithm and Turkey's improved Hough transform fitting algorithm. The proposed wheel profile monitoring method was validated on a high-precision motion platform. As the rotational speed increased, the radius deviation of the wheel was reduced from ±0.56 μm to ±0.24 μm, and the maximum radius deviation was reduced from less than 0.13% to less than 0.03% of the nominal radius. The graphite plate replica method was also used to verify the accuracy of the measurements, and the results demonstrated the feasibility and accuracy of the method for the rapid, non-contact measurement and monitoring of grinding wheel profiles. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.
