AD  - Central Public Works Department, Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs, Government of India, New Delhi, 110011, India
AD  - Department of Architecture and Design, Jordan University of Science and Technology, Irbid, 22110, Jordan
AB  - Green facades are increasingly promoted as climate-adaptive envelope strategies for moderating solar heat gains, yet their annual energy performance in composite climates remains uncertain due to pronounced seasonal thermal reversals and the widespread use of static vegetation assumptions in simulation studies. This study evaluates the energy performance of a direct green facade incorporating deciduous climbing vegetation through a multiscale framework that integrates long-term field monitoring with seasonally calibrated building energy simulation. A one-year experimental campaign was conducted on paired vegetated and bare brick masonry walls of a residential building in New Delhi, India, capturing summer, monsoon, winter, and extreme weather conditions, including heatwave events with ambient temperatures exceeding 47 °C. Field measurements indicate substantial summer thermal buffering, with reductions of 3.2 °C in exterior surface temperature and 2.2 °C in indoor air temperature relative to a control wall, while winter impacts were minimal due to reduced foliage density. These observations were used to calibrate a seasonally responsive EnergyPlus model incorporating monthly variation in vegetation shading effectiveness, achieving high predictive accuracy for indoor air temperature during summer conditions (R2 = 0.97). At the building scale, the validated model projects annual cooling energy savings of approximately 8% for west-facing facades and up to 25% when applied across all orientations, with limited and localized winter heating penalties. The results demonstrate that explicitly accounting for vegetation seasonality is essential for reliably quantifying the annual energy implications of green facades in composite climates and provides climate-sensitive guidance for facade greening strategies under conditions of strong seasonal thermal contrast. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
