AD  - Yibin Research Institute, Southwest Jiaotong University, Yibin, 644000, China
AB  - Adaptive façades present significant potential for improving building energy efficiency. Existing thermotropic (TT) smart facade designs mainly employ single-layer structures, which limit their optimization in terms of thermal insulation, and occupant views. Research on structural optimization of TT façades still remains limited, and current TT parallel slats configurations may still lead to uneven daylight distribution under low solar altitude angles and can suffer from structural instability. To address these limitations, this study proposes a novel thermotropic honeycomb transparent insulation material (TT HC-TIM) system, integrating a honeycomb TT structure into conventional double-glazing façade. The optical and thermal performance of the system was investigated using ray-tracing and CFD simulations, while its annual thermal and daylighting performance were analyzed through EnergyPlus simulations. In addition, the numerical model was validated by experimental measurements. Results indicate that TT HC-TIM provides improved daylight uniformity while maintaining outward visibility, effectively modulates direct solar radiation, and reduces convective heat transfer through cavity subdivision. The system presents high solar transmittance about 70% at low incident angle even under the translucent state, showcasing good view connection for indoor occupants. It also exhibits the lowest U-value compared with double-glazing and current TT façade systems, reduces monthly heat loss by over 30%, and balances solar gains to support both cooling and heating demands, demonstrating high potential for enhancing indoor thermal environment and daylighting quality. Copyright © 2024. Published by Elsevier Ltd.
