AD  - Innovation Institute for Sustainable Maritime Architecture Research and Technology, Qingdao University of Technology, Qingdao, 266033, China
AB  - As global warming escalates, dynamic photovoltaic facade systems (DPFS) offer a promising but underexplored pathway to enhance building energy efficiency. However, existing studies have predominantly focused on static photovoltaic designs. This study aims to explore the energy performance and optimal design strategies of DPFS for an office building in Changshu, China. The novelty is to comprehensively investigate DPFS with varying adjustment frequencies (monthly and hourly) and tracking strategies (vertical-axis, horizontal-axis, and dual-axis). The energy performance of various optimized DPFS was simulated and compared based on minimal net energy consumption under consistent structural and lighting constraints. The main findings indicate that: (1) the three DPFS achieved minimal energy consumption when the azimuth angles on different facades ranged from -45° to 10° (east), -14° to -9° (south), and -14° to 45° (west), and the altitude angles ranged fro. m 21° to 26° (east, west) and from 24° to 34° (south); (2) the dual-axis system performed best when PV area was unconstrained, while the horizontal-axis system excelled under equal-area conditions; (3) the horizontal-axis system proved to be the most economically viable. These findings provide a systematic theoretical exploration for the design and application of photovoltaic shading systems in the hot-summer-cold-winter climate zone. © 2025
