AB  - Against the backdrop of worsening urban thermal environments, shaded spaces play a significant role in improving outdoor thermal comfort. This study, conducted in Xiamen, China—a city in the hot-summer and warm-winter climate zone—employed a comparative experimental approach to examine differences in outdoor thermal adaptation between participants in building-shaded and unshaded environments. Drawing on the Griffiths method, an outdoor thermal sensation prediction model and a thermal comfort range prediction model were developed, and both models were validated through field surveys. The results show that the Griffiths constant (G) is higher under shaded conditions than under unshaded ones, and that shaded environments also exhibit greater psychological tolerance, with demand factors below 1 in summer and the transitional season, and approaching 1 in winter. Neutral temperature increases significantly with rising outdoor mean radiant temperature of the open area (Tmrt_OA) under both conditions, but the growth patterns differ. Furthermore, based on experimental results, an outdoor thermal sensation model and a thermal comfort range prediction model were established by incorporating shade-seeking probability and a demand factor. Compared to traditional linear models, the proposed models significantly improve the accuracy of predicting thermal sensation and thermal comfort ranges. © 2025
