AD  - IERC, International Energy Research Centre, Tyndall National Institute, University College Cork, Cork, Ireland
AB  - In addition to admitting daylight into a building, a mechanically ventilated double-skin façade can heat, ventilate and insulate to meet occupant comfort requirements. Generally, in the Irish temperate climate, buildings require ventilation in summer and heating at other times of the year. Control strategies for a mechanically ventilated double-skin façade that achieve this have been investigated using experimentally validated building simulations. A ventilation-prioritized strategy ventilates the building during the day and provides thermal insulation at night. A heating-prioritizing strategy provides heating during the day and reverts to solely thermally insulating at night. The applicable duration of the ventilation-prioritized strategy becomes progressively shorter towards winter. For a single-room building, applying these seasonal prioritized modes on a month-by-month schedule enabled a particular type of mechanically-ventilated double-skin façade to reduce energy consumption by 36 % compared with the same room with a full-height double-glazed window. © 2025
