AD  - Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, 48109, MI, United States
AD  - College of Architecture and Environmental Design, California Polytechnic State University, San Luis Obispo, 93405, CA, United States
AB  - A novel framework for comprehensive evaluation of the design space and potential performance of environmentally responsive adaptive façade concepts is presented and evaluated. In particular, this framework seeks to quantify the potential impacts of a concept on building performance beyond what can be derived from individual case studies. This approach derives fundamental environmental conditions to represent the broad spectrum of potential use cases, and evaluates optimal design and performance for all combinations of these fundamental conditions. To demonstrate generalization capabilities, a novel approach is presented to estimate optimal design and performance for a wide range of use cases. A kirigami-inspired double skin façade concept for solar tracking and adaptive ventilation is used as a case study to demonstrate the capability of the approach. By evaluating the results of the generalized analysis, the optimal designs and potential performances for each combination of fundamental conditions are identified. Furthermore, the features of the optimal designs that are common across the range of environmental variations can also be determined. Capabilities of this approach are further demonstrated with the approach shown to estimate the optimal design parameters and potential performance of any given use case. Through the analysis of two prototypical buildings, it is shown that the performance, as measured by net energy generation, and the optimal set of component design parameters can be estimated for specific cases. However, it is noted that the accuracy of both estimates could improve by expanding the set of fundamental environmental conditions considered. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
